/*
# Floating

The base of every overlay in the library — `Popover`, `Sheet`, `Dialog`, `Toast`, and the
menus — and of a program's own (`class Viewer extends Dialog [ … ]`). It is declared where it
belongs, in its owner, and paints above everything in its context: in the overlay layer the
nearest `Window` provides, else in the App's own layers — overlays in the order they open,
toasts above them.
It carries, once, what every overlay needs:

- **Opening and closing** — `show()` and `close()`; `shown` says whether it is open now. A
  closed overlay builds no view of its own until it is first shown.
- **Motion** — it animates in and out on `openT` (0 closed, 1 open), and stays built until
  its closing motion lands: closing stops it taking input at once.
- **Input** — `pressPolicy` and `scrollPolicy` say what a press and a scroll aimed past it do
  while it is open. An outside press that dismisses closes it.
- **Keys and focus** — while open it holds the keyboard (a key it uses does not also reach
  the program's `KeyListener` members, and the page's bindings stand down); Esc closes the open
  overlay nearest the focus; one that takes focus focuses its first stop on opening and gives
  focus back on closing.
- **Anchoring** — `anchorBox(v)` is a view's box in the overlay's frame, for an overlay that
  opens next to something; `toLayer(v, x, y)` maps a point the same way, through a window
  turned or scaled; `keepOnApp(x, y, w, h)` moves a box back onto the App.
- **Its colours are theme tokens** — the panel of a `Dialog`, `Sheet` or `Popover` paints the
  theme's `surface` (a `Menu`'s, `menuFill` else `surface`; a `Toast`'s, `tooltipFill`), and the
  backdrop `scrimFill` at `scrimOpacity`. One overlay of its own colour is given a theme with
  the token changed: `theme = { ({ ...provided("theme"), surface: #111111 }) }`.
- **Forms** — it is a boundary: a field inside belongs to a `Form` inside it, not to one
  around the place it is declared.

```declare
App [ width = 420, height = 220,
    Button [ x = 20, y = 20, label = "Show", onClick() { app.note.show() } ],
    note: Popover [ anchorTo = { app.children[0] as View },
        Text [ text = "A popover is a Floating." ]
        ]
    ]
```

## _notice
**Internal.** A notice (a toast): it paints in the layer above every other overlay. Other
overlays share one layer and stack in the order they open, so a menu opened from a dialog
paints above it.

## shown
Whether it is open now. Open it with `show()` and close it with `close()` — or make it
state: `shown = { app.editing }` with `input(open: boolean) { app.editing = open }`, and it
opens and closes as the state does (SwiftUI's `isPresented`).

## present
Whether it is built and drawn: open, or closing until its motion lands. Content built
fresh for each opening reads it, so it stays through the closing motion instead of
emptying the panel as it goes: `entry: Form [ exists = { app.formSheet.present } ]`.

## _live
**Internal.** Whether the open work has run (its layer, its keyboard hold, focus) — what
`_sync()` compares with `shown`, so the work runs once per change, whoever made it.

## openT
The open blend, 0 closed and 1 open, sprung — what a subclass draws its motion from.

## host
The view it paints in, chosen when it opens.

## _hostW
**Internal.** The width of the layer it paints in: its window's, or the browser window's — not the
App's, which on a page that scrolls is as tall as the page.

## _hostH
**Internal.** The height of the layer it paints in: its window's, or the browser window's.

## opener
The view it was opened for, when it was opened for one.

## pressPolicy
What a press **aimed past** it does while it is open. `dismiss`: it hears `onOutsidePress` and
the press goes no further (a menu). `dismissAndDeliver`: it hears `onOutsidePress` and the press
goes on to what was pressed (a popover). `block`: the press goes nowhere and it stays (a
dialog). `ignore`: the press goes on as if it were not there (a toast). Open overlays are
walked top first: one that lets the press go on hands it to the next one down.

## scrollPolicy
What a scroll — wheel or touch — aimed past it does while it is open: `block` (nothing behind
it scrolls), `allow`, or `dismiss` (it hears `onOutsidePress` and the scroll goes on).

## takesFocus
Whether opening moves focus into it (its first stop) and closing gives it back — to where
`restoresFocusTo` says, else to the view that had it, or, when that view is gone or hidden
by then, to the next Tab stop after its place.

## _sharesProgram
**Internal.** Whether, open in a host's layer (a program running in a window, say), it is shown at its
program's scale and turn there — as a menu or a popover is. `false` for what attaches to
the window itself, edge to edge (a `Sheet`). `true` by default.

## _hostedLayer
**Internal.** Whether the layer it opened in is a host's — its program runs inside another.

## scale
Its program's scale where it opens in a host's layer (`_baseScale`) — a subclass multiplies
its own motion into it.

## rotation
Its program's turn where it opens in a host's layer (`_baseRotation`), in degrees.

## _baseScale
**Internal.** Its program's scale relative to the layer, when it opens in a host's; else 1.
A subclass's own motion multiplies it.

## _baseRotation
**Internal.** Its program's turn relative to the layer, in degrees, when it opens in a host's;
else 0.

## _takeProgramTransform()
**Internal.** Sets `_baseScale` and `_baseRotation` as it opens.

## input()
**Where opening and closing go** — the value pattern, as a control's `value` goes through
its `input`. The default writes `shown`. To make whether it is open your own state,
constrain `shown` and override this to write that state, so its own closings (Esc, a press
past it) reach it: `shown = { app.editing }, input(open: boolean) { app.editing = open }`.
Constraining `shown` without it is a compile error.
An override must deliver: write the state `shown` follows, or — with `shown` not
constrained — call `super.input(open)`; otherwise `show()`, `close()`, Esc and a press past
it do nothing. To act on closing, override `closed()` instead.

## _sync()
**Internal.** Runs the open or close work when `shown` has changed — after `show()`,
`close()`, or a change of the state `shown` follows.

## _opening()
**Internal.** The open work: picks its layer, builds it, holds the keyboard, and takes
focus if it takes focus.

## _closing()
**Internal.** The close work: lets the keyboard go, gives focus back, calls `closed()`.

## onRetire
An overlay that goes while it is open leaves the overlays' stack and lets the keyboard
go, as a close would. An override calls `super.onRetire()`.

## show()
Opens it, through `input(true)`: picks its layer, builds it, holds the keyboard, and takes
focus if it takes focus.

## showFor()
Opens it for `v` — `opener` becomes `v`.

## _holdFocusItself()
**Internal.** Focuses the overlay itself when it takes focus and holds nothing focusable,
so Tab cannot reach what is behind it.

## focusFirst()
Where the focus goes when an overlay that `takesFocus` opens: the first view inside that
takes focus, else the overlay holds the focus itself, so Tab has nowhere to go behind it and
Esc still closes it. An override that may find nothing to focus calls `super.focusFirst()`
for that case.

## close()
Closes it at once, through `input(false)` (it stops taking input); it is torn down when its
closing motion lands.
An override calls `super.close()`. Work for every closing belongs in `closed()`: the state
`shown` follows can close it without coming through here.

## closed()
Called once it is closed — override to act on closing. It runs before focus is given back,
so focus moved here (to what the closing created) stays where it was put.

## _sendHold
**Internal.** What a `Form` inside, while it sends through its source, holds the overlay's
own ways of closing with — `SendHold`.

## escape()
What Esc does when this is the open overlay nearest the focus — closes it; a dialog picks its
cancel button instead.

## keyBindings
Escape runs `escape()` while it is shown — and, while a `Form` inside is sending through its
source, waits for the answer instead, as a press outside and a swipe do. The program's own
`close()` never waits. Focus inside the overlay walks to it before
anything outside; with focus outside it (a combobox's list), its keys come to it first.

## _keyEscape()
**Internal.** Escape: `escape()`, while it is shown.

## isTop()
Whether it is the topmost open overlay — a dialog's Return picks its default only when it is.

## anchorBox()
A view's box in this overlay's frame: `{ x, y, w, h }`.

## toLayer()
A point in `v`'s own frame, in the frame of the layer this paints in: `{ x, y }`. Exact
through a turned or scaled window, and from a program hosted in a window into its host's.

## _screen()
**Internal.** The App the layer belongs to — the host's, for a program whose overlays open in its host's
window. An overlay may leave its window, never the screen.

## _toScreen()
**Internal.** A point in `v`'s own frame, in the screen's frame.

## _through()
**Internal.** A point `(px, py)` through a `transformTo` similarity.

## keepOnApp()
Where a box at `(x, y)`, `w` by `h`, in the layer's frame, must be to stay on the screen
with 4 to spare: `{ x, y }`, moved as little as it can be. An overlay inside a window may
hang past the window's edge, but not off the screen.

## _layerFor()
**Internal.** The layer it opens in: the nearest `Window`'s; else, in a program hosted in a
window whose island provides `overlays`, that window's; else the App's — a toast always the
App's.

## _strata()
**Internal.** The App's overlay layers, made the first time an overlay opens.

## onOutsidePress
An outside press (or, with `scrollPolicy = "dismiss"`, an outside scroll) asked it to close.
It closes — unless a `Form` inside is sending through its source: then it waits for the
answer (which would otherwise land nowhere), saying "Still sending" once to a screen reader.
An override that closes regardless says so by calling `close()`.

## _savedFocus
**Internal.** The view that had focus when it opened, given focus back when it closes.

## _savedParent
**Internal.** The parent of `_savedFocus` when it opened: its place, should it be gone by
the close.

## _savedAt
**Internal.** The index `_savedFocus` held among `_savedParent`'s children.

## _savedRing
**Internal.** Whether `_savedFocus` showed its focus ring when it opened: focus given back
shows it again only then — a dismissal is not navigation.

## visible
Follows `present`: it is drawn while open, and while its closing motion runs.

## paintsIn
Follows `host`: it paints in the layer it opened into.

## outsidePress
**Internal.** `pressPolicy` while it is open, `none` once it closes — a closing overlay takes no presses.

## scrollBelow
**Internal.** `scrollPolicy` while it is open, `none` once it closes.

## _form
**Internal.** an overlay is a form boundary — a control in it belongs to a `Form` inside the
overlay, not to one around where the overlay is declared.

## _field
**Internal.** the same boundary for a `Field`.
*/

include [ "overlaystrata.declare" ]

use [ OverlayStrata ]

class Floating [
    ignoreLayout = true,

    // an overlay is a boundary for forms: what it holds belongs to a Form
    // inside it, never to one around the place it is declared

    _form: any = null,
    _field: any = null,

    // the overlay a Form inside it holds open while it sends: its own ways of
    // closing — Escape, a press outside, a swipe — wait for the answer, which
    // would otherwise land nowhere (key-bindings.md; data: DataSource.failure)

    _sendHolder: SendHold [ open = { classroot.shown } ],
    _sendHold: any = { this._sendHolder },
    _waitsOnSend() -> boolean { return this._sendHolder.waits() },

    _notice: boolean = false,
    shown: boolean = false,
    present: boolean = false,
    openT: number = 0,
    host: View? = null,
    _hostW: number = { this.host != null ? this.host.width : app.hostWidth > 0 ? app.hostWidth : app.width },     // a window's, else the browser window's
    _hostH: number = { this.host != null ? this.host.height : app.hostHeight > 0 ? app.hostHeight : app.height },
    opener: View? = null,
    pressPolicy: "dismiss" | "dismissAndDeliver" | "block" | "ignore" = "dismiss",
    scrollPolicy: "block" | "allow" | "dismiss" = "block",
    takesFocus: boolean = false,
    _savedFocus: View? = null,
    _savedParent: View? = null,
    _savedAt: number = -1,
    _savedRing: boolean = false,
    _hostedLayer: boolean = false,
    _sharesProgram: boolean = true,
    _baseScale: number = 1,
    _baseRotation: number = 0,

    visible = { present },
    paintsIn = { host },
    scale = { _baseScale },
    rotation = { _baseRotation },
    outsidePress = { shown ? pressPolicy : "none" },
    scrollBelow = { shown ? scrollPolicy : "none" },

    // it animates in and out, and is torn down when the closing motion lands

    _motion: Spring [ attribute = openT, to = { classroot.shown ? 1 : 0 }, stiffness = 520, damping = 40, epsilon = 0.01,
        onStop() { if (!classroot.shown) classroot.present = false }
        ],

    // the App's layers, made once and kept above everything the App holds

    _strata() -> OverlayStrata {
        let s: OverlayStrata | null = null
        for (const c of app.children) { if ((c as any)._isOverlayStrata === true) s = c as any }
        if (s == null) return app.createView("OverlayStrata", ({ })) as any
        s.raise()
        return s
        },

    // the nearest Window's layer, else the App's; a toast is the program's,
    // always the App's

    _layerFor(s: OverlayStrata) -> View {
        if (_notice) return s.notice
        const own = provided("overlays", null) as View | null
        _hostedLayer = false
        if (own != null) return own
        const hosts = hostProvided("overlays", null) as View | null   // the nearest layer up the chain, in a host
        if (hosts == null) return s.overlays
        _hostedLayer = true
        return hosts
        },

    // in a host's layer it is shown as its program is: the program's scale and
    // turn relative to the layer (the layer brings the window's own)

    _takeProgramTransform() {
        if (!_hostedLayer || !_sharesProgram || host == null) { _baseScale = 1; _baseRotation = 0; return }
        const t = app.transformTo(host)
        _baseScale = t.scale
        _baseRotation = t.rotation * 180 / Math.PI
        },

    // `shown` is state: the verbs below deliver through `input` (the value
    // pattern), and a use site may instead constrain it to state of its own —
    // `shown = { app.editing }` with `input(open: boolean) { app.editing = open }`
    // — so Esc and a press past it still close it. Either way the open and
    // close work runs when `shown` changes (sync), whoever changed it.

    input(open: boolean) { shown = open },

    _live: boolean = false,

    // The open and close work runs from this member, not from the overlay's own
    // handlers, so a subclass's or a use site's `trackChanges`, `onChange` or
    // `onInit` — written for anything of its own — cannot drop it.

    _watch: Node [
        open: boolean = { classroot.shown },
        trackChanges = ["open"],
        onInit() { if (open) afterSettle(() => classroot._sync()) },
        onChange() { classroot._sync() }
        ],

    _sync() {
        if (shown && !_live) { _live = true; this._opening() }
        else if (!shown && _live) { _live = false; this._closing() }
        },

    show() {
        if (!shown) this.input(true)
        this._sync()
        },

    _opening() {
        const s = this._strata()
        host = this._layerFor(s)
        this._takeProgramTransform()
        present = true
        s.opened(this)
        if (pressPolicy != "ignore") Keyboard.navClaim(this, true)
        if (takesFocus) {
            _savedFocus = Focus.getFocus()
            _savedParent = _savedFocus != null ? _savedFocus.parent as View | null : null
            _savedAt = _savedParent != null ? _savedParent.childViews.indexOf(_savedFocus) : -1
            _savedRing = _savedFocus != null && Focus.byKeyboard()
            afterSettle(() => this.focusFirst())
            }
        },

    showFor(v: View) {
        opener = v
        this.show()
        },

    // nothing inside to focus (a full-screen picture): the overlay holds focus
    // itself, so Tab has nowhere to go behind it and Esc still closes it

    focusFirst() { if (!Focus.focusIn(this)) this._holdFocusItself() },

    _holdFocusItself() {
        focusable = true
        Focus.focus(this)
        },

    close() {
        if (shown) this.input(false)
        this._sync()
        },

    _closing() {
        this._strata().closedIt(this)
        Keyboard.navClaim(this, false)
        // back to where it was, or where restoresFocusTo says — unless the focus has
        // gone on somewhere since; to the next Tab stop when what had it is gone.
        // A turn after what the closing delivers (a dialog's picked, a menu's
        // pick — themselves a turn later): a pick that removes what had the
        // focus (the row a "Delete?" was about) has removed it by then
        if (_savedFocus != null || restoresFocusTo != null) afterDelay(0, () => afterDelay(0, () => {
            const f = Focus.getFocus() as View | null
            let inside = false
            for (let p = f; p != null; p = p.parent) if (p == this) inside = true
            if (f == null || inside || Focus.holds(this)) Focus._giveBack(this)
            else { _savedFocus = null; _savedParent = null }
            }))
        this.closed()
        },

    closed() { },

    // gone while open (its row rebuilt, its view discarded): off the stack and
    // the keyboard released, or both would hold the discarded overlay for good

    onRetire() {
        if (!_live) return
        _live = false
        for (const c of app.children) { if ((c as any)._isOverlayStrata === true) (c as any).closedIt(this) }
        Keyboard.navClaim(this, false)
        },

    isTop() -> boolean { return this._strata().top() == this },

    anchorBox(v: View?) -> object {
        if (v == null) return ({ x: 0, y: 0, w: 0, h: 0 })
        const o = this.toLayer(v, 0, 0)
        const k = host != null ? v.transformTo(host).scale : 1
        return ({ x: o.x, y: o.y, w: v.width * k, h: v.height * k })
        },

    // frames: a view's point mapped into the layer's — exact through a turned
    // or scaled window, and from a program hosted in a window into the host's
    // layer (transformTo crosses the island)

    toLayer(v: View, px: number, py: number) -> object {
        return this._through(v.transformTo(host != null ? host : this._screen()), px, py)
        },

    // the screen: the App the layer belongs to — the host's, for a program
    // whose overlays open in its host's window. A menu may leave its window,
    // never the screen.

    _screen() -> View {
        if (host == null) return app
        let n: View = host
        while (n.parent != null) n = n.parent as View
        return n
        },

    _toScreen(v: View, px: number, py: number) -> object { return this._through(v.transformTo(this._screen()), px, py) },

    _through(t: object, px: number, py: number) -> object {
        const a = t.rotation
        return ({ x: t.x + t.scale * (px * Math.cos(a) - py * Math.sin(a)), y: t.y + t.scale * (px * Math.sin(a) + py * Math.cos(a)) })
        },

    // the box's corners on the screen give the shift that keeps it there; the
    // shift maps back into the layer's frame

    keepOnApp(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number) -> object {
        const scr = this._screen()
        if (host == null) return ({ x: Math.max(4, Math.min(scr.width - w - 4, x)), y: Math.max(4, Math.min(scr.height - h - 4, y)) })
        const out = host.transformTo(scr)
        const cs = [this._through(out, x, y), this._through(out, x + w, y), this._through(out, x, y + h), this._through(out, x + w, y + h)]
        const minX = Math.min(...cs.map((c) => c.x))
        const maxX = Math.max(...cs.map((c) => c.x))
        const minY = Math.min(...cs.map((c) => c.y))
        const maxY = Math.max(...cs.map((c) => c.y))
        const dx = maxX > scr.width - 4 ? scr.width - 4 - maxX : 0
        const dy = maxY > scr.height - 4 ? scr.height - 4 - maxY : 0
        const sx = minX + dx < 4 ? 4 - minX : dx
        const sy = minY + dy < 4 ? 4 - minY : dy
        if (sx == 0 && sy == 0) return ({ x: x, y: y })
        const back = scr.transformTo(host)
        const p0 = this._through(back, cs[0].x, cs[0].y)
        const p1 = this._through(back, cs[0].x + sx, cs[0].y + sy)
        return ({ x: x + p1.x - p0.x, y: y + p1.y - p0.y })
        },

    onOutsidePress() { if (!this._waitsOnSend()) this.close() },

    escape() { this.close() },

    // Esc closes it: a binding, so the nearest open overlay to the focus takes
    // it, and one Esc closes one (key-bindings.md)

    keyBindings = { [ ({ key: "Escape", command: "_keyEscape" }) ] },

    _keyEscape(e: KeyEvent) -> boolean {
        if (!shown) return false
        if (this._waitsOnSend()) return true
        escape()
        return true
        }
    ]


/*
# SendHold

**Internal.** What an overlay (a `Floating` — `Sheet`, `Dialog`, `Popover` — or a `Window`)
holds while a `Form` inside it is sending through its `source`: the overlay's own ways of
closing — Escape, a press outside, a swipe — wait for the answer, which would otherwise
land nowhere. The program's own `close()` never waits, and closing lets go.

## hold()
**Internal.** A form inside starts (`on`) or ends sending.

## waits()
**Internal.** A dismissal asks: true while held, saying "Still sending" once.
*/

class SendHold extends Node [
    open: boolean = false,
    holds: any[] = [],
    held: boolean = { this.holds.length > 0 },
    _told: boolean = false,
    hold(by: any, on: boolean) {
        const was = holds.includes(by)
        if (on && !was) holds = [...holds, by]
        else if (!on && was) holds = holds.filter((h) => h !== by)
        if (holds.length == 0) _told = false
        },
    waits() -> boolean {
        if (!held) return false
        if (!_told) { _told = true; Accessibility.announce("Still sending") }
        return true
        },

    // closed (by the program — its own ways wait), what held it lets go: a
    // form discarded mid-send holds nothing the next time it opens

    trackChanges = ["open"],
    onChange(e: ChangeEvent) { if (!open) { holds = []; _told = false } }
    ]
